# Finite positive-ladder approximation at finite laboratory time

## Conditional construction and the obstruction at a fixed cap

A finite-dimensional GKSL approximation can be constructed for a finite-sector,
finite-neighborhood COMPLETE native instrument, with an explicit retained-output
trace bound. The construction needs no commensurability of the original spectra:
rounding is part of the approximation. It conserves the ROUNDED modeled energy
exactly, including absorbing refusal. It does not exactly conserve the original
unrounded energy distribution. A small bound for original mean-energy error is
available separately.

A prescribed finite cap cannot guarantee arbitrary accuracy solely by taking
a finer mesh: actual ideal leakage outside that cap can be nonzero. Measuring
outside the cap gives a trace-norm lower bound twice that leakage against ANY
normalized output strictly supported in the cap (with refusal in an orthogonal
flag). Thus mesh refinement removes discretization error, not physical cap
error. The sufficient construction below is conditional on a displayed cap
margin inequality. Failure of that conservative inequality is not a no-go;
one may need a larger priced cap or a sharper leakage estimate.

## Target and hypotheses

Take a finite direct sum Hsys of all physically needed head/history/Record
sectors, total dimension d. Source Hamiltonians A_s are bounded self-adjoint
operators on this finite matter-neighborhood model. Complete edge/sign columns
K_e have norm1, at most D outgoing edges per sector, and gamma is fixed. The
full-line target jumps are their exact spectral energy lifts, with common free
Hamiltonian A direct_sum + EB. Assume the complete-native local transfer bound
 ||K_e A_s-A_target K_e||<=c
uniformly; c=Delta+t when the native surviving-Hamiltonian identity applies.
Let Lambda=gamma D, lambda=Lambda T, and
 P(lambda)=lambda+(3/2)lambda²+(1/3)lambda³.

This theorem starts from a finite-neighborhood target whose free Hamiltonian
and sector dimensions are actually specified. If distant free-Hamiltonian
couplings remain in the target, replacing the whole system by a finite patch
requires a separate locality comparison. Merely having local jump support does
not license deleting those couplings. Native tensor-product placement and
locality of the physical K_e remain hypotheses, not consequences of d.

Initial battery is the normalized sine on[b,b+w], with b>0. A requested cap C
has u=C-b-w>0. The input system/reference state is arbitrary and uncorrelated
with this fixed battery. All results retain the battery at output.

## Rounding and a positive ladder embedding

Choose a mesh delta>0. Round every eigenvalue of every A_s to the nearest point
of delta Z, keeping the same projectors; fix one tie convention. Call the result
A_s^delta. Uniformly,
 ||A_s-A_s^delta||<=delta/2.
Round FULL sector energies once, including any declared fuel contribution;
never add the fuel gap a second time. If exact fuel spacing Delta is required,
choose delta=Delta/m and round only the matter spectra; this preserves fuel
spacing. The simplest general construction below allows full-sector rounding,
so its exact conservation law is explicitly for A^delta.

Use ladder centers E_j=(j+1/2)delta, j in Z. Isometrically embed |j> as the
normalized constant function on[j delta,(j+1)delta) in L2(R,dE). Let J_delta
be this embedding, P_delta its range projection, and
 EB^delta(E)=delta(floor(E/delta)+1/2).
Then ||EB-EB^delta||<=delta/2 on the full continuous space. Integer translations
m delta preserve the step-function subspace, and EB^delta preserves it as well.
Because rounded system-energy differences are integer multiples of delta,
every rounded lifted jump preserves that subspace EXACTLY. Its restriction is
an ordinary shift on the integer ladder with the same system spectral matrices.

For C=M delta, restrict to j=0,...,M-1. These are strictly positive battery
levels delta/2,...,C-delta/2. The embedded cells fill[0,C]; there is no negative
half-cell at the origin. Using integer-centered cells without correcting the
zero-energy cell would not have this property. Given an upper cap C, exact
alignment can always be obtained by choosing delta=C/M with sufficiently large
integer M. If exact Delta commensurability is also demanded, use an aligned
cap at or below C and price its slightly smaller upper margin separately.

## Initial packet error

Let beta_delta=P_delta beta/||P_delta beta||. On each cell the mean-zero
Poincare inequality gives
 ||beta-P_delta beta||<=delta ||beta'||/pi=delta/w.
The sine extended by zero belongs to H1, including when b or b+w lies inside
a cell. Thus its normalized projection has pure-state trace distance
 || |beta><beta|-|beta_delta><beta_delta| ||_1
 =2||beta-P_delta beta||<=2delta/w.                      (1)
Take delta<w so the projection is nonzero. The projected packet's LADDER
energies lie in[b-delta/2,b+w+delta/2]; its embedded continuous support lies
in[b-delta,b+w+delta]. Require delta<=min(b,u), so it fits strictly inside the
chosen positive cap. No product/reset condition is imposed after initialization.

## Full-line dynamical comparison before any cap

For an edge/sign COLUMN and arbitrary tau, rounding gives
 ||exp(-i tau Aout)K exp(i tau Ain)
   -exp(-i tau Aout^delta)K exp(i tau Ain^delta)||
 <=delta |tau|.                                         (2)
The two exponential errors each cost(delta/2)|tau|. This estimate preserves
all signs in one column. It does not multiply the bound by their number.
Completeness is exact for the rounded lift, because the same rounded input
and output Hamiltonians appear across its sign column.

First compare exact and rounded jumps while keeping the continuous free EB.
On initial Fourier band |tau0|<=Tcut, free transport visits tau0+t, so the
Duhamel jump cost over[0,T] is at most
 4 Lambda delta (T Tcut+T²/2).                           (3)
Changing free A to A^delta costs at most delta T in diamond norm. Changing free
EB to EB^delta costs another delta T, because their difference is bounded by
delta/2 even though EB itself is unbounded. The latter comparison is an
unrestricted bounded-Hamiltonian-perturbation estimate; it does NOT assume
pure Fourier translation after EB is rounded.

For the original sine packet,
 eta(Tcut)<=32pi/[3(w Tcut)^3], Tcut>=sqrt(2)pi/w.
The retained-output band truncation costs4sqrt(eta), as in the fixed-time
locality proof. Combining it with(1)–(3) gives the unconditioned full-line bound
 E_disc=2delta/w +4 Lambda delta(T Tcut+T²/2)+2delta T
                      +4sqrt(eta(Tcut)).                 (4)
This compares the continuous target with the embedded UNBOUNDED integer-ladder
model initialized in beta_delta. The intermediate continuous-space comparison
can start from beta; only afterwards is its initial input replaced by beta_delta.
This ordering avoids assuming that rounding EB preserves Fourier marginal
translation. Every triangle step is CPTP and uses one retained battery.

## Cap completion and repeated refusal

For the rounded full-line column,
 ||[EB^delta,V_e^delta]||
 =||K_e A_s^delta-A_target^delta K_e||<=c+delta.          (5)
The equality follows directly from integer shifts; the inequality adds the two
rounding errors to the original transfer defect. It does NOT require the
rounded surviving Hamiltonian to commute with K_e.

Let P_M project to the M positive ladder levels. For each edge take the combined
accepted column S_e=P_M V_e^delta P_M, and ONE absorbing refusal complement
F_e=(I_cap-S_e*S_e)^(1/2). All these are finite matrices. The cap commutes with
A^delta+EB^delta, the lift intertwines that energy, and F_e commutes with the
source rounded total energy. Use the same source Hamiltonian on its refusal
copy. The resulting finite GKSL process is CPTP and exactly preserves the
ROUNDED total-energy distribution, despite nonzero refusal.

Put b_delta=b-delta/2, u_delta=u-delta/2, and
 B_delta=(c+delta)² (b_delta^-2+u_delta^-2) P(lambda).
The repeated-cap quantum union bound, including Poisson null ticks and actual
interleaved free evolutions, gives
 refusal probability by T<=min{1,4B_delta},
 full capped-versus-full-line ladder channel distance
                     <=min{2,4sqrt(B_delta)}.             (6)
Here the ladder free Hamiltonian commutes with its energy projection. Null
ticks cause no additional refusal on accepted inputs. No Fourier-translation
argument is applied after capping;(6) uses the separate commutator/union proof.

Therefore the finite positive-ladder process, embedded in a common output
space with the continuous target and orthogonal refusal labels, satisfies
 ||Finite_T-Continuous_T||_diamond,fixed beta
 <=min{2,E_disc+4sqrt(B_delta)}.                          (7)
The diamond norm has arbitrary system/reference input but the declared initial
battery fixed in each map (beta versus its projected preparation). No conditional
rare-success normalization is hidden in(7).

## Explicit resource choice for a target epsilon

For 0<epsilon<1, choose
 Tcut=max{sqrt(2)pi/w, [8192pi/(3epsilon²)]^(1/3)/w}.
Then4sqrt(eta)<=epsilon/4. Define
 Aerr=2/w+4Lambda(T Tcut+T²/2)+2T.
Choose delta_max=min{w/2,b,u,1,epsilon/(4Aerr)} and an integer
 M>=ceil(C/delta_max), setting delta=C/M.
This makes the discretization part excluding the Fourier tail<=epsilon/4.
Check the explicit remaining condition
 4(c+delta)sqrt(P(lambda)[(b-delta/2)^-2+(u-delta/2)^-2])
 <=epsilon/2.                                          (8)
When(8) holds, (7)<=epsilon. All constants and the dimension are explicit.
If(8) fails, mesh refinement alone is not certified to attain epsilon at that
cap. A simple sufficient margin criterion, using delta<=min(1,b,u), is
 8(c+1)sqrt(P(lambda)(b^-2+u^-2))<=epsilon/2.
For symmetric margins b=u=m, it suffices to price
 m>=16sqrt(2)(c+1)sqrt(P(lambda))/epsilon, C=2m+w.
These are conservative resource THEOREMS, not a sweep or retuning of any
frozen scientific experiment. At T=0 the cap cost vanishes and no positive
margin enlargement is demanded by this dynamical term.

An accepted system dimension d with ONE absorbing copy needs at most2dM
Hilbert dimensions; distinct edge refusals can share that copy while their
Lindblad/environment labels stay separate. It can be stored in
ceil(log2(2dM)) abstract qubits. This is a dimension count, not a locality-
preserving native encoding. Keeping a given q_sys-qubit native representation
instead gives q_sys+ceil(log2 M)+1 storage qubits with unused code states.
The external Markov reservoir/unraveling is still supplied; a finite GKSL
state space is not a finite CLOSED unitary dilation for arbitrary time.

## Original energy error, separately from trace error

Under the cell embedding, ||A-A^delta||<=delta/2 and
||EB-EB^delta||<=delta/2. Since the finite process exactly conserves the rounded
energy, its drift in ORIGINAL modeled total-energy mean is at most2delta
between its own initial and final states. This holds also in refusal sectors,
whose original/rounded source Hamiltonians are paired consistently.

The original sine and projected preparation have supports contained in
[b-delta,b+w+delta]. Centering EB at b+w/2 and applying(1) bounds their initial
battery mean difference by delta+2delta²/w. The matter input is identical.
Thus relative to the continuous target's conserved original total-energy mean,
 |Etotal,finite(T)-Etotal,target(T)|<=3delta+2delta²/w.    (9)
This is not inferred from a trace bound against an unbounded observable; it
uses exact rounded conservation and bounded energy-operator differences.

If R_A=(max spectrum A-min spectrum A)/2 across the finite system/refusal
sectors, battery mean error also satisfies
 |EBfinite(T)-EBtarget(T)|
 <=3delta+2delta²/w + R_A times the trace bound in(7).   (10)
R_A is a finite-neighborhood energy scale, not an assumed global volume-uniform
constant. Exact original energy-distribution conservation is not claimed;
(9) is a mean estimate only.

## What remains unsolved

The result supplies explicit finite matrices, a positive finite ladder, a
CPTP absorbing-refusal GKSL law and a retained-output finite-time error bound.
It does not supply native one-site/Z3 admissibility, spatially local realization
of spectral rounding within a patch, a local bath, entropy/memory reset,
preparation or indefinite renewal. The integer ladder can require many levels;
no optimal resource claim is made. A whole-lattice application requires the
separate finite-neighborhood comparison and its surviving ambient-algebra
assumptions. Feedback/noncomplete KJ columns are outside this construction.
